WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.880
HELGA: Hi, my name is Helga and I’m from Iceland.&nbsp;
Election day in Iceland is always on a Saturday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:14.460
and the government encourages people to vote.&nbsp;
Election day is also always in the summertime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.080
so no one is weather-trapped or can’t&nbsp;
make it because of bad weather conditions.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:24.060
FATIMAH: Hi everyone, my name is Fatimah.&nbsp;
I’m from Ecuador and I just want to let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:29.640
you know a little bit about how voting is on&nbsp;
election day. It’s actually mandatory to vote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.960
which I think is great, and on&nbsp;
that day you get an appointment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.780
on a location that you need to vote&nbsp;
on. Normally it happens on Sundays.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:44.940
You don’t really get a lot of lines because it’s&nbsp;
already pre-registered with your ID number. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:50.520
polls happen continuously around the day, and&nbsp;
then you get the results at the end of the day.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:01:03.240
SIERRA: Voting is one of the few things that I&nbsp;
actually remember really clearly from my early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.040
childhood. Our polling place was in my elementary&nbsp;
school, which was closed for the occasion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.140
so this meant it was all dim and quiet.&nbsp;
My mom would take me into the booth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:18.660
she would close the curtain behind us. And&nbsp;
there was this air of excitement, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:23.940
and seriousness in there. She’d tell me which&nbsp;
candidates to choose and I would flip the heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:29.340
switches and then at the very end we would&nbsp;
pull the lever together. In the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:37.260
only 55 percent of the eligible population voted&nbsp;
in the 2016 election. In terms of a test grade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:42.540
that’s a hard fail, and one that puts us&nbsp;
far behind most of the democratic world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:47.700
There’s a certain irony there. The U.S.&nbsp;
is the world’s oldest modern democracy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:52.980
Over the centuries it inspired dramatic&nbsp;
democratic shifts across the globe - and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:59.220
its constitution was regularly used as a model in&nbsp;
the process. And yet now, after teaching so much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:06.780
the U.S. finds itself with a lot to learn on a key&nbsp;
democratic function. My name is Gabrielle Sierra,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:18.977
and this is Why It Matters. Today, why the United&nbsp;
States lags on voter turnout and what can be done.

00:02:18.977 --> 00:02:21.060
Laura Flanders:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:26.928
The most important election in a&nbsp;
generation is fast approaching.

00:02:26.928 --> 00:02:30.600
TODAY Show: America’s most basic&nbsp;
civic duty can and will go on.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.307
Chasing News:Election day.

00:02:31.307 --> 00:02:31.962
KRIS 6 News: Election day.

00:02:31.962 --> 00:02:33.360
Associated Press: Election day in America.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:39.480
BECKER: There is no question that when more&nbsp;
citizens of a democracy are invested in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:43.680
democracy through the act of voting that democracy&nbsp;
is more legitimate, it's more functional.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:49.800
I'm David Becker. I'm the executive director and&nbsp;
founder for the Center for Election Innovation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.880
and Research in Washington, DC. We're&nbsp;
a nonpartisan nonprofit that works with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.300
election officials all over the country&nbsp;
and all across the political spectrum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.780
to make elections that are more secure&nbsp;
and accessible for all American voters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.720
And I think it's also important to note that the&nbsp;
adversaries of democracy—autocracies around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:12.720
world—want citizens and democracies to disengage.&nbsp;
They want them to believe that they cannot trust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.860
their election outcomes, that their elected&nbsp;
leaders are illegitimate, and the democracy is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:21.240
not working as a form of government. And that's&nbsp;
why it's more important than ever, that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:26.580
world's democracies fight against these things and&nbsp;
work to get their citizens engaged and involved.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:32.040
DIXON: Gabrielle, I think voter turnout&nbsp;
is a sign of people's engagement with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:37.140
democratic politics. So by itself, it's a&nbsp;
key indicator of the health of a democracy.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:41.400
SIERRA: This is Rosalind Dixon,&nbsp;
professor of law at the University&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:47.100
of New South Wales in Sydney - which is&nbsp;
coincidentally my study abroad alma mater.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.240
She is also a visiting&nbsp;
professor at Harvard Law School.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:56.520
DIXON: But it's also really important to&nbsp;
making sure that politics functions to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:00.600
meet the needs of citizens. If people don't&nbsp;
turn out to vote, their voices aren't heard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:06.420
their needs are less likely to be factored in, in&nbsp;
the democratic process. And that's particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:11.880
important if there's a systematic skew, whereby&nbsp;
some voters time after time, don't show up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:17.760
politicians start counting on that, and they don't&nbsp;
design policies around those citizens' needs.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:26.280
CLARKE: I like to just do that reality check&nbsp;
and really situate the United States in a global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:33.360
framework. And I think it's sobering to see that,&nbsp;
in so many respects, we deem the United States to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:39.180
be one of the leading democracies across the globe&nbsp;
but we're not when it comes to voter participation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:47.460
and voter turnout by any stretch. My name is&nbsp;
Kristen Clark. I'm the President and Executive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:55.800
Director of the Lawyers Committee for Civil Rights&nbsp;
Under Law. And that's because we make it really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:01.380
hard. We make it really hard for people to get&nbsp;
registered, we make it hard for people to vote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:07.980
we make it hard for them to stay on the rolls,&nbsp;
and we have very fractured systems where we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:15.240
got 50 states that all have their own separate&nbsp;
rules and approaches. And, you know, it's unlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:21.480
other major democracies around the globe that&nbsp;
have more uniform systems, and that are really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:28.560
about making it easy for people to participate.
 
BECKER: In a presidential election we'll normally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.120
see about 60 percent of eligible voters&nbsp;
participate. That's the high watermark in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:38.520
general for voter turnout. In other elections,&nbsp;
such as midterm elections, the elections for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:42.900
Congress in between presidential elections,&nbsp;
we’ll normally see turnout around 40 percent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:47.820
give or take. And then in a primary election it&nbsp;
might only be about 25 or 30 percent. And since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:52.620
those are split between the parties, Democratic&nbsp;
and Republican parties, in order to win a primary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:57.540
election, it might actually be a very small&nbsp;
number of voters who have actually selected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:01.800
the candidate that is going to be one of the two&nbsp;
who will become president. And that's exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:08.340
what we saw in 2016. Only about fifteen million&nbsp;
people in the Republican Party voted for Donald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:14.940
Trump to be their nominee. And yet, because of how&nbsp;
primaries work, that was enough votes for him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:19.800
advance to the general election as the Republican&nbsp;
nominee. And to be perfectly honest, Secretary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:24.360
Clinton got about the same number of votes and&nbsp;
achieved the Democratic nomination. So this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:29.520
where people opting out of voting, whether by&nbsp;
choice or just inertia, can make a big difference

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:35.340
SIERRA: According to a 2018 PEW&nbsp;
study, Belgium has the world’s best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:40.440
rate of voter participation, with 87&nbsp;
percent. Sweden is right behind with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:46.320
nearly 83 percent. Denmark and Australia&nbsp;
also top the charts. The United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:54.480
again, sits at 55 percent, placing 26th out of&nbsp;
32 advanced democracies. There are no simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:07:00.420
answers about why. But part of the problem is&nbsp;
that many Americans don’t even attempt to vote.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:08.040
BECKER: I think, for many voters, they just don't&nbsp;
know what the point of their vote is, why does it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:13.500
matter? And we've had a hard time articulating&nbsp;
why their vote matters. Your vote doesn't matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:17.220
because you're likely to cast the one ballot&nbsp;
that's going to be the deciding vote in a race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:21.240
almost nobody is going to ever vote in a race&nbsp;
where one vote is going to choose. It happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:26.100
but it happens extremely rarely. If you&nbsp;
ask voters in the United States if they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:30.420
registered and if they vote, more will say&nbsp;
they are registered and more will say they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:35.700
vote than actually do. In other words, some are&nbsp;
lying. And some people get depressed about that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:39.720
I actually think that's a good sign, it means that&nbsp;
people already know that something they should do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:43.260
They don't need to be convinced that voting is a&nbsp;
good thing. They don't need to be convinced that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:47.220
participation is a good thing. But they're not&nbsp;
doing it, and that tells me that we just need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:51.900
get them more engaged. You know, why do one-third&nbsp;
of the voters who participate in the presidential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:56.340
election not participate two years later in a&nbsp;
congressional election? We don't really have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.340 --> 00:08:00.720
good answer for that and that's literally tens of&nbsp;
millions of people. About forty to fifty million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:07.020
people didn't participate in 2014 after they did&nbsp;
vote in 2012. They were mostly registered. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:10.800
mostly knew about the election. They knew how to&nbsp;
vote. They'd use the technology, but they didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:14.520
show up. These are all things we really need to&nbsp;
figure out and we don't have great answers on.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:20.640
SIERRA: There is no single explanation for&nbsp;
non-voters, and experts like David Becker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:26.820
have dedicated their careers to understanding the&nbsp;
question. That said, there are some trends. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:32.700
Knight Foundation’s 100 Million Project found&nbsp;
that 38 percent of non-voters feel that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:38.880
election systems are rigged, that their vote has&nbsp;
no impact, and that the U.S. government does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:44.400
represent the will of the people. Additionally,&nbsp;
non-voters reported that they don’t engage as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:49.121
actively with news, and feel that they are&nbsp;
not equipped with enough information to vote.

00:08:49.121 --> 00:08:52.800
Knight Foundation: I don’t think I’m gonna vote&nbsp;
because I’m not informed on anything. I haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:57.720
done any research so I feel like my vote’s gonna&nbsp;
be wasted. I feel like being young and voting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:01.980
people will judge you for it because you’re&nbsp;
not educated enough about the whole voting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:06.360
process. I don't think my vote matters. I think&nbsp;
the whole thing is predetermined. The idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:08.940
voting is kinda like a, like a scheme.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:13.500
SIERRA: Studies like the 100 Million&nbsp;
project point to deep psychological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:19.560
and educational barriers to voting. Beyond&nbsp;
these, according to experts, there are also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:24.780
several systemic factors that can stand in&nbsp;
the way even after someone decides to vote.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:29.280
One of these problems is that in the&nbsp;
United States voting can be...complex.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:36.960
CLARKE: We get a lot of calls to our 866 Our&nbsp;
Vote hotline from people asking basic things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:42.840
like “What's the deadline for registering to&nbsp;
vote?” “How do I register?” “Can you register to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:49.500
vote online?” “Can I get access to an absentee&nbsp;
ballot? Am I eligible?” I think that we can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:55.200
a much better job at voter education. But then&nbsp;
there's a different form of voter education and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.200 --> 00:10:00.720
that's really understanding what you need to&nbsp;
cast an effective ballot. Are there going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:07.020
be three or four ballot amendments that require&nbsp;
that you read through a long narrative and vote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:14.340
yes or no? There's this phenomenon in our country&nbsp;
where we see huge drop offs as people make their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:21.540
way down the ballot. In a presidential election&nbsp;
year, you may see very high participation rates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:26.820
at the presidential level, maybe for your state&nbsp;
governor, and then just a huge drop off as you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:33.120
get down to things like the State Commissioner on&nbsp;
Education and the Tax Assessor and the Sheriff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:38.520
these are positions that absolutely&nbsp;
have impact on our day to day lives.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:44.640
SIERRA:Right. So I probably shouldn't be standing&nbsp;
outside my polling place, Googling what each of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:49.680
these positions, even does, let alone the&nbsp;
name of the candidates that are up for it.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:55.380
CLARKE: I've found myself in that position,&nbsp;
too, where I'm whipping out my phone, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.380 --> 00:11:01.260
at the ballot box here in DC. But some states&nbsp;
actually impose time limits on how long you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:06.960
stay inside and vote. So you only have so much&nbsp;
time to do that research on the spot. Yeah.

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:15.180
SIERRA: Sometimes when you see media coverage of&nbsp;
long lines for early voting, let's say in Georgia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:21.000
or elsewhere, you might hear people say, you&nbsp;
know, “This is democracy in action! These people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:26.580
are patiently waiting to have their voices heard!”&nbsp;
You know, what are the problems with those lines?

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:33.660
CLARKE: They discourage people. I went to my&nbsp;
bank the other day and there was a line that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:40.020
stretched halfway down the block. I turned around,&nbsp;
I didn't have what looked to be like an hour, hour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:46.020
and a half to wait that line out. So these lines&nbsp;
that you see during our elections, to me, are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:53.700
heartbreaking. I understand that kind of reaction&nbsp;
of it being a sign that democracy is alive and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.700 --> 00:12:00.120
know, people are participating in high numbers,&nbsp;
and that's part of the story. But the other part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:05.760
when you when you look closely, is that oftentimes&nbsp;
there are breaks in the system. Georgia is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:12.240
example of that. Georgia was a mixed tale of&nbsp;
people really turning out historic numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:18.780
but we dealt with the complaints. People who told&nbsp;
us about sites that just were not up and ready in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:25.080
the morning, poll books that were not working&nbsp;
so people couldn't get checked in properly. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:30.840
you know, officials often are not coming through&nbsp;
for voters in the way that we need them to.

00:12:31.940 --> 00:12:35.400
SIERRA: Do you think that there's an argument that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:40.380
making voting easier also makes the&nbsp;
process more susceptible to fraud?

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:46.800
CLARKE: I've got a good friend, Justin Levitt,&nbsp;
who teaches out in California who determined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:53.040
that you've got a better chance of being struck&nbsp;
by lightning than seeing fraud play out in our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.040 --> 00:13:01.140
elections. These claims about vote fraud are&nbsp;
really used and touted as a narrative to drive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:06.720
fear, to create the impression that we need&nbsp;
to have really strict rules and burdensome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:12.780
requirements that make it super hard for people&nbsp;
to vote to prevent fraud. There really just is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.780 --> 00:13:20.100
no evidence of fraud being a widespread problem&nbsp;
in our country and it's just a distraction from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:27.600
the real problem, which is that there are far too&nbsp;
many Americans who don't vote, who can't vote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:33.920
who are unable to vote, because of the enormous&nbsp;
obstacles and burdens that we put in their way.

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:38.360
SIERRA: These obstacles&nbsp;
are part of a long history.

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:45.360
CLARKE: Voting in our country has always&nbsp;
been hotly contested ground. Our nation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:50.340
has a fraught history of denying&nbsp;
Black people access to the ballot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:56.760
denying women access to the ballot for&nbsp;
a period of time, and we've had a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.760 --> 00:14:03.980
struggle to try and push for all communities&nbsp;
to gain full access to the franchise.

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:10.320
BECKER: I mean, it is literally fifty-five&nbsp;
years ago that the Voting Rights Act of 1965&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:14.580
was passed. And that was passed&nbsp;
because a very large percentage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:19.040
of American citizens were not seeing&nbsp;
the promise of their vote realized.

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:24.600
SIERRA: The Voting Rights Act was signed into&nbsp;
law at the height of the Civil Rights Movement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:31.380
and it prohibited racial discrimination in voting.&nbsp;
In 2013, the Supreme Court struck down parts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:36.840
the law. Some saw this as a victory for states’&nbsp;
rights. Many others have seen it differently.

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:45.660
CLARKE: The court basically brought to a grinding&nbsp;
halt a core piece of the Voting Rights Act that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:51.900
applied to parts of our country that had very long&nbsp;
and egregious histories of voting discrimination.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:58.200
Many states in the south like Mississippi,&nbsp;
Louisiana, Alabama, South Carolina, Florida,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:04.920
Texas, were included as well and a number of&nbsp;
other states. But before they could make changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:10.620
to voting, before they could purge people from the&nbsp;
rolls, before they could shut down polling sites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:16.620
they had to get federal review, largely carried&nbsp;
out by the Justice Department. And that federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:24.420
review process looked to see whether the voting&nbsp;
change would harm or worsen the position of Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:31.680
voters, Latino voters, and other voters of color.&nbsp;
And that provision of the law was remarkable in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:39.600
blocking and deterring a lot of voter suppression&nbsp;
and a lot of voting discrimination. And since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:44.880
that Supreme Court ruling, we've literally just&nbsp;
seen the floodgates of voter suppression open up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:51.840
across our country. And it's resulted in a lot&nbsp;
of setbacks, a lot of reversals in progress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:58.560
and a lot of litigation by civil rights&nbsp;
organizations, like my own. We are we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:04.680
fighting to make sure that all communities&nbsp;
are able to have voice in our democracy.

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:10.200
SIERRA: When it comes to overall turnout&nbsp;
in the United States, issues of race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:16.860
gender and vote suppression are very real factors.&nbsp;
But there’s a lot of other important factors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:22.020
too. There’s the complex psychology of what&nbsp;
actually leads someone to exercise their vote.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:28.140
And a vast system of differing local rules&nbsp;
to navigate. It’s a lot! And there are some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:33.420
great resources in the show notes if you want&nbsp;
to dig deeper. But, it’s also worthwhile to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:39.120
outward at what other countries are doing that&nbsp;
seems to be working for them. Take… Australia.

00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:47.040
DIXON: Australia has a record of close to 100%&nbsp;
turnout, which is one of the things we're very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:52.320
proud of. Voting in Australia is compulsory.&nbsp;
And what that means is that you have a legal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:57.660
obligation to vote in every election, state,&nbsp;
federal and local. You have a choice between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.660 --> 00:17:03.120
paying a penalty and showing up to vote. And the&nbsp;
penalty is actually not very high. It's around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:07.800
$100. And you can write a letter and explain why&nbsp;
you can't pay it or why you had a good reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:11.940
for not voting. So, you know, many countries,&nbsp;
indeed dozens of countries around the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:17.760
have mandatory voting. But, they have high turnout&nbsp;
and much higher turnout than the US, but something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:22.620
quite, distinctly lower than Australia. So&nbsp;
the mandate alone is not the explanation.

00:17:22.620 --> 00:17:27.720
SIERRA: So it's not just the mandatory&nbsp;
voting law. How would you categorize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:31.380
the major factors that have led to&nbsp;
such a high turnout in Australia?

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:35.340
DIXON: Well, I think voting in&nbsp;
Australia is relatively easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:39.840
it's made to be as enjoyable as&nbsp;
possible as a kind of social practice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:46.800
and there's a strong social norm that voting&nbsp;
is expected as a responsibility of citizenship.

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:51.360
SIERRA: So, Australia’s recipe&nbsp;
for turnout actually has four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:57.120
ingredients. Mandatory voting, sure.&nbsp;
But also, easy voting. A shared sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:01.640
of civic responsibility and something&nbsp;
you might not expect. Enjoyable voting.

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:06.600
SIERRA: When we say enjoyable,&nbsp;
what do you mean? Like, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:10.860
fun to vote? Or it's a big party,&nbsp;
like what do you mean, enjoyable?

00:18:10.860 --> 00:18:13.920
DIXON: Closer to a party. Now, you&nbsp;
know, in COVID, there are no parties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:18.480
right? But the tradition in Australia is you show&nbsp;
up at your local polling station on a weekend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:23.400
there are people there handing out how to vote&nbsp;
cards, but there's also like a hotdog stand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:29.340
and a cake stall and you see people from the&nbsp;
neighborhood. So it's closer to a potluck. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:34.320
there's definitely a sense that you go up, you can&nbsp;
take your kids and your dog and you can see your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:39.480
neighbors and chat; not just, you know, show up&nbsp;
in a line. And that's part of what makes it easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:42.900
right? You show up and you're like, this is&nbsp;
actually kind of pleasant. I'll see my neighbors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:49.200
I'll have a chat and it's not a chore. It's&nbsp;
almost like Fourth of July in terms of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:54.720
degree to which people have made it fun and you&nbsp;
know, something that's part of the social fabric.

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:58.200
SIERRA: Hotdogs aside, to many Americans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:03.180
a mandatory voting law could feel like a&nbsp;
constraint on freedom. But many Australians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.180 --> 00:19:07.740
have come to see the law as more of a&nbsp;
signpost than a constraint, something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:12.860
that builds voting into the social fabric by&nbsp;
establishing a sense of shared responsibility.

00:19:12.860 --> 00:19:20.040
DIXON: So law can do two things. One is it can&nbsp;
really coerce people to do the right thing when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.040 --> 00:19:28.080
they want to do otherwise, right, it can be a&nbsp;
really important kind of moral and coercive force.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:33.420
But sometimes law just coordinates our behavior.&nbsp;
So whether we drive on the left or the right hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:38.160
side of the road, something I've had to remind&nbsp;
myself of from time to time living in the US and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:43.080
Australia, is not something anyone wants to get&nbsp;
wrong. No one wants to be in a head on collision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:48.120
right? So the law tells us to drive on the&nbsp;
left or the right hand side. But it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:52.920
really needing to coerce us, it's just sending&nbsp;
a signal to people of what's expected so we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:58.080
all coordinate and get it right. And on one&nbsp;
view, that's what mandatory voting laws do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:02.580
in Australia and elsewhere, they don't actually&nbsp;
force us to do the right thing. They just send&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:07.440
a signal to citizens that everyone's going to&nbsp;
vote. And so the social norm is we all show up.

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:10.680
SIERRA: And this brings us to&nbsp;
the final ingredient, “ease.”

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:17.100
DIXON: I think, actually, all of them are mutually&nbsp;
reinforcing, but I think it's the most important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.100 --> 00:20:23.760
so it is always on a weekend. And they make&nbsp;
sure it's not on a weekend that clashes with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:28.920
some major event. So you cannot have it on the&nbsp;
day of a major sporting final, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.920 --> 00:20:32.940
because then people would be outraged that they&nbsp;
have to miss the sporting finals. There's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:39.180
good pre polling, there's good postal voting.&nbsp;
So basically, you almost never wait more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:45.960
about 15 to 30 minutes. And there's a sense&nbsp;
in which that is linked to pretty effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:51.540
controls on voter suppression. So they don't&nbsp;
make it really hard to prove identity and to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:57.660
get the vote counted. And for regular full&nbsp;
time workers, it doesn't clash with work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.660 --> 00:21:02.220
you don't have to, you know, have a boss who&nbsp;
supports you, you can just show up to vote.

00:21:02.220 --> 00:21:07.740
SIERRA: So beyond grabbing rights,&nbsp;
why does high turnout even matter?

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:13.320
DIXON: One of the things that happens when&nbsp;
you have a system where everyone votes is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.320 --> 00:21:20.520
politicians modify their own behaviors in their&nbsp;
own policies to respond to the likelihood that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:26.280
there will be large turnout, and that they are&nbsp;
appealing to the median voter and not simply their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:32.220
base. And that's one of the best parts of a system&nbsp;
of compulsory voting is it drives the kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:38.820
policy behaviors from major parties that are truly&nbsp;
about serving ordinary folks and the median voter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:46.140
not the extreme polls of our polity, and only&nbsp;
seeking to appeal to a party space. I think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.140 --> 00:21:50.760
important to note the similarities between the&nbsp;
US and Australia, but also the idea that every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:55.080
country is ultimately different. Australia&nbsp;
is not the US, the US is not Australia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.080 --> 00:22:01.560
we're a 10th of the size, we have more of a mix&nbsp;
of American and social democratic traditions. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:08.160
I do think there's really important similarities.&nbsp;
We are a federal system that borrowed heavily from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:14.940
the United States, we have the same common law&nbsp;
tradition and set of constitutional guarantees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:20.700
around judicial independence. And we have&nbsp;
a degree of cultural similarity, which is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:26.400
we're much more pro-free market and individual&nbsp;
responsibility than Canada, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:31.260
right? So I always think of Australia as kind&nbsp;
of midway between Canada and the US culturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:36.840
we have more of a welfare state, we have more&nbsp;
of a sense of collective responsibility for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.840 --> 00:22:41.340
people who fall through the cracks. But we're&nbsp;
more free market and entrepreneurial. And we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.340 --> 00:22:46.320
more American in our sense of the frontier&nbsp;
and a sense of entrepreneurship. But you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:50.820
we're not the same. And no country is&nbsp;
the United States. You cannot find a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:55.800
country that's identical. So, you know, I think,&nbsp;
Gabrielle, you've spent some time in Australia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.800 --> 00:23:00.420
you would see it as similar and different.&nbsp;
But I would say the Australian model has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.420 --> 00:23:05.160
lots of downsides in other contexts, but&nbsp;
this is one of our absolute best exports.

00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:13.380
SIERRA: So Australia is full of good ideas. And&nbsp;
it seems like they’re working. But what seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.380 --> 00:23:19.140
easy and natural in one country can be quite&nbsp;
difficult to implement in another. After the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:23.520
break, we turn to David Becker to discuss&nbsp;
the feasibility of adopting Australia’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.520 --> 00:23:29.520
examples. But first, a few more global Why It&nbsp;
Matters listeners on voting around the world.

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:35.100
REBECCA: Hi my name is Rebecca, and I am&nbsp;
a Singapore citizen and I voted for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:40.020
first time in our general election this year.&nbsp;
Voting is fairly straight-forward in Singapore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.020 --> 00:23:44.691
voting is mandatory so we always&nbsp;
get a turnout of about 97 percent.

00:23:44.691 --> 00:23:48.840
SPEAKER: Voting in Kenya can be very&nbsp;
intimidating. We have a very long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.840 --> 00:23:54.060
history of election-related violence. You’re&nbsp;
likely to encounter very very long lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:54.060 --> 00:23:58.800
normally when you vote you are also&nbsp;
asked to give your fingerprints so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.800 --> 00:24:04.500
anybody who votes has blue ink on&nbsp;
their hands for a couple of days.

00:24:07.640 --> 00:24:14.460
SIERRA: Hello listeners! So if you dig all&nbsp;
the questions we ask in Why It Matters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:18.780
you are going to love this other podcast&nbsp;
brought to you by our friends over at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:25.320
Pushkin Industries. It’s called Solvable. Kind&nbsp;
of a natural fit. Solvable is a weekly podcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.320 --> 00:24:31.260
where Malcolm Gladwell, Anne Applebaum, and&nbsp;
Jacob Weisberg ask how we can find solutions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.260 --> 00:24:36.180
to some of the biggest issues of our time.&nbsp;
You’ll hear from David Baltimore on AIDS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:48.660
Sal Khan on basic education, and Roseanne Hagerty&nbsp;
on homelessness. These problems can seem too big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:55.380
or too complicated to fix. But in the hands&nbsp;
of the right people -- activists, scientists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:55.380 --> 00:25:01.920
policymakers and politicians at the top of their&nbsp;
fields -- there are ways to solve them. And that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.920 --> 00:25:06.540
the hopeful message of Solvable. Listen and&nbsp;
subscribe now in your favorite podcasting app.

00:25:08.180 --> 00:25:16.080
SPEAKER: In the UK until 2011, a Prime Minister&nbsp;
could call an election whenever they wanted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:23.880
Since then though, we have had scheduled general&nbsp;
elections that take place every five years on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.880 --> 00:25:30.660
first thursday in May. One odd quirk about the&nbsp;
UK system, is that you don’t need ID to vote.

00:25:30.660 --> 00:25:34.140
SPEAKER: In Switzerland we’ve&nbsp;
had mail-in voting for years now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:38.400
I haven’t voted in person in, probaby,&nbsp;
25 years. We are also automatically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:44.240
registered to vote when we turn 18,&nbsp;
so the whole process is super easy.

00:25:44.240 --> 00:25:50.160
SIERRA: And we are back. So, as promised,&nbsp;
let’s ask David Becker about some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:53.580
ingredients that combine to make&nbsp;
Australian voting such a success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.580 --> 00:25:55.980
and see if he thinks they can work in the States.

00:25:56.660 --> 00:26:04.140
SIERRA: So, first one is instituting a&nbsp;
national voter holiday or weekend voting.

00:26:04.140 --> 00:26:07.980
BECKER: One of the things I worry about is&nbsp;
there's other things that go along with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:07.980 --> 00:26:12.900
national holiday — there are school closures,&nbsp;
public transportation office often is on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:18.420
reduced schedule, and hourly employees who are&nbsp;
often the people who we most want to hear from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:18.420 --> 00:26:22.800
whose voices are not as loud as they could&nbsp;
be, they often might be incentivized to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:27.120
more by earning more wages on a national&nbsp;
holiday than they would on an ordinary day.

00:26:27.120 --> 00:26:32.760
SIERRA: How about a national voting registry,&nbsp;
one of these magic lists that you mentioned?

00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:37.800
BECKER: That's very common in most established&nbsp;
democracies in the world. We have never had that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:41.640
in the United States. And in fact,&nbsp;
we rely upon individual state voter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.640 --> 00:26:46.260
lists. And that's actually even fairly a recent&nbsp;
development, up until about fifteen years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:46.260 --> 00:26:51.060
many states still retained lists solely at&nbsp;
the county or local level. And so having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.060 --> 00:26:56.400
fifty state voter lists, and often trying to&nbsp;
integrate that with a motor vehicles agency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:56.400 --> 00:27:01.380
which is probably one of the data sources that has&nbsp;
the most people in it can be really helpful, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.380 --> 00:27:06.180
it's not as good as a unified citizen registry.&nbsp;
So I think there's a couple of barriers to this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:06.180 --> 00:27:11.280
one coming from the right and one coming from the&nbsp;
left. I think the right is reasonably concerned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.280 --> 00:27:19.260
about issues related to privacy and federalism&nbsp;
in creating a single national master database of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:19.260 --> 00:27:25.860
all citizens. And I think the left is worried&nbsp;
that such a database could be used to cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:31.500
difficulties or even deport people who may not&nbsp;
be citizens, whether they're here legally or not.

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:36.840
SIERRA: Okay last one, what about mandatory&nbsp;
voting as something that the US could implement?

00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:41.520
BECKER: There are some who talk about some kind&nbsp;
of compulsory voting requirements in the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:41.520 --> 00:27:47.220
States and whether it's possible. Again, I'm not&nbsp;
against it. I think it's highly unlikely. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.220 --> 00:27:52.320
we have some things like the First Amendment that&nbsp;
possibly collide with that. Although there’ve been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:56.700
some suggestions about ways to avoid First&nbsp;
Amendment free speech-compelled speech kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:56.700 --> 00:28:00.660
of issues, and they're thoughtful&nbsp;
approaches. But I will tell you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:04.260
I just don't think there's any political will&nbsp;
to move in that direction right now. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:04.260 --> 00:28:09.000
think it's highly likely that we'll see that&nbsp;
in the United States anytime soon. And I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:15.180
you know, I'm going to focus my efforts&nbsp;
on making compulsory voting moot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.180 --> 00:28:19.560
because people want to vote. And that's what I'd&nbsp;
like to see done more. And I think, and as I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.560 --> 00:28:22.260
we're still learning about that so much,&nbsp;
that's going to take a lot of time.

00:28:22.260 --> 00:28:27.120
SIERRA: There’s no silver bullet. In&nbsp;
Australia, these policies are part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:32.520
a social fabric that has grown organically&nbsp;
around them. America has its own culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:37.200
and its own norms, and that means that&nbsp;
no single policy from another country can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:42.420
fix the problem. If you try to import the rule&nbsp;
without the social norms, you’re in for trouble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:50.280
And that leads to a deeper question - how do you&nbsp;
change a voting culture? Not just for a single,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:54.180
dramatic election, but in the&nbsp;
long term, across the spectrum?

00:28:54.920 --> 00:29:00.000
BECKER: I think there's pretty good evidence that&nbsp;
civic education in the United States has gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:04.980
down over the course of the last forty to fifty&nbsp;
years. This has been in conjunction with efforts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:11.160
to delegitimize our democratic system. I mean,&nbsp;
there has been efforts to portray our system of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:11.160 --> 00:29:15.600
democracy as simply a game with winners and losers&nbsp;
and they're playing the game against each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:19.920
and they're trying to get whatever advantage&nbsp;
they can. That's not a very constructive way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:24.900
to run a democracy. This is more than a game.&nbsp;
So we have to take it a little more seriously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:29.940
and reject efforts to portray this as a horse&nbsp;
race. You know, we're in litigation right now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.940 --> 00:29:35.160
where we see a lot of rules being changed in&nbsp;
the election process by people who think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:40.500
certain rules will benefit or harm their party&nbsp;
prospects. Look, that's just simply wrong. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:46.680
we have to be willing to accept a world where&nbsp;
our candidates might lose and accept that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.680 --> 00:29:53.820
is an important democratic lesson. If we have 100&nbsp;
percent turnout and my preferred candidate loses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:57.380
I consider that a big win. And we're&nbsp;
not even close to there right now.

00:29:57.380 --> 00:30:04.680
CLARKE: I think about 2020, I mean, this is a&nbsp;
moment where people are, marching and protesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:14.100
in ways that we've not seen previously for a&nbsp;
response to the crisis of police violence and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:20.160
racial violence that has long gripped our nation.&nbsp;
These marches and protests, I think, are signs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:20.160 --> 00:30:28.080
of hope for long overdue reform. But I wonder if&nbsp;
people also realize that their votes are another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:28.860 --> 00:30:35.760
powerful weapon for achieving reform on those&nbsp;
issues. So you know, do people understand that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:35.760 --> 00:30:41.040
the district attorney is an elected position,&nbsp;
right, and that district attorney is the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:41.040 --> 00:30:47.220
that's looked to to decide whether or not to&nbsp;
prosecute an officer who used deadly force without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:47.220 --> 00:30:54.360
basis on an unarmed African American? Do people&nbsp;
understand that the mayor is the one that often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.360 --> 00:31:02.520
gets to decide who serves as police chief? So when&nbsp;
I think about voter education, I'm thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:02.520 --> 00:31:07.980
getting people access to accurate information.&nbsp;
And I'm also thinking about empowering people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:07.980 --> 00:31:15.660
so that they understand what their votes mean.&nbsp;
And, you know, I often hear from people as I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:15.660 --> 00:31:23.580
travel and do this work that, who's occupying 1600&nbsp;
Pennsylvania Avenue doesn't impact whether or not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:23.580 --> 00:31:32.460
I can get food on the table, or whether I'm able&nbsp;
to get a job, and I do think that for those folks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:32.460 --> 00:31:38.040
there is a little bit of work to do in&nbsp;
understanding how power manifests itself.

00:31:38.040 --> 00:31:43.200
SIERRA: In an ideal democracy, everyone&nbsp;
understands the power of their vote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:48.180
and everyone uses it. And look, of&nbsp;
course there are no ideal democracies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:48.180 --> 00:31:54.300
each comes with its flaws. But it’s worth noting&nbsp;
that the same countries that top the voter turnout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:54.300 --> 00:32:00.960
list - Australia, Denmark, Norway, and Sweden&nbsp;
for example- also top the most trusted global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:00.960 --> 00:32:06.480
indices for quality of life and democratic&nbsp;
freedoms. Correlation is not causation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:12.360
but the trendline is there. And that may be&nbsp;
because high turnout forces politicians to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.360 --> 00:32:17.880
come up with policies that address the needs of&nbsp;
large groups of people, rather than the needs of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:17.880 --> 00:32:24.060
their followers. But to get there, you need an&nbsp;
electorate with a shared educational baseline.

00:32:24.060 --> 00:32:28.080
BECKER: I think we're doing better in&nbsp;
many ways than we've ever done before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:29.100 --> 00:32:35.820
But I think we need to educate our children and&nbsp;
educate our media about what the end goal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:41.280
democracy is. The end goal of democracy is not a&nbsp;
period of time during which you can stand on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:41.280 --> 00:32:46.560
top pedestal and be declared the winner. The end&nbsp;
goal of democracy is to better living conditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:46.560 --> 00:32:51.900
and the environment for our fellow citizens.&nbsp;
And if democracy doesn't serve that role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:51.900 --> 00:32:55.560
then we have to ask ourselves, why are we&nbsp;
engaged in it? And that's, of course, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:55.560 --> 00:33:02.040
our adversaries want. There are people who are&nbsp;
in power solely because they subvert any efforts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:02.040 --> 00:33:08.180
of democracy in their country. And they benefit&nbsp;
from that, and so when we doubt that, they win.

00:33:08.180 --> 00:33:11.960
SIERRA: What reforms would you most like to see?

00:33:11.960 --> 00:33:16.920
BECKER: So, I think one of the things I'm&nbsp;
seeing from this election cycle is theres&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:16.920 --> 00:33:20.940
such an effort to try to game the system&nbsp;
and change the rules close to an election,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:20.940 --> 00:33:25.560
when the political dynamics are largely set.&nbsp;
And what I'd like to see in an off year when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:25.560 --> 00:33:29.940
the political dynamics are still very vague,&nbsp;
we're not sure who's going to be benefited by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:29.940 --> 00:33:34.800
certain things, an effort to bring Republicans&nbsp;
and Democrats together to try to establish some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:34.800 --> 00:33:40.500
common rules for the process. I think our&nbsp;
decentralization is good in many ways. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:40.500 --> 00:33:45.900
I do think we probably need to do more to&nbsp;
establish a national floor. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:45.900 --> 00:33:49.920
what's a minimum that every state can do?&nbsp;
Every state should probably offer no-excuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:56.280
mail voting options. Every state should probably&nbsp;
offer some degree of early voting. Every state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:56.280 --> 00:34:01.440
should allow for correction of problems with&nbsp;
ballots to some degree. And probably, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:01.440 --> 00:34:06.540
every state should also have a standard for when&nbsp;
ballots are received. And one of the problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:06.540 --> 00:34:09.960
we face right now is there's no federal law&nbsp;
that says these things, and it's not in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.960 --> 00:34:13.800
Constitution. And so there's a lot of vagueness,&nbsp;
and I think one of the things I'd like to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:13.800 --> 00:34:19.200
during the off years is an effort to try to&nbsp;
resolve some of these issues and avoid some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:19.200 --> 00:34:24.180
of this litigation by the parties who are trying&nbsp;
to game the system one way or the other. The rules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:24.180 --> 00:34:29.220
should be set well before we start playing,&nbsp;
and we're not really quite there. We probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:29.220 --> 00:34:33.680
experience a lot more pre- and post-election&nbsp;
litigation than almost any other country.

00:34:33.680 --> 00:34:40.320
CLARKE: I would love to see same day voter&nbsp;
registration. There are, you know, a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:48.000
people who I think, miss out on the opportunity&nbsp;
to vote because of just really restrictive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:53.880
timelines for getting registered. In states&nbsp;
that have same day registration like Minnesota,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:53.880 --> 00:35:00.420
those states enjoy some of the highest turnout&nbsp;
and participation rates in the country. I'd love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:00.420 --> 00:35:05.940
to see what it would look like if we had voting&nbsp;
on weekends in our country, when people have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:05.940 --> 00:35:12.360
little bit more flexibility and latitude. I'd love&nbsp;
to see automatic voter registration. I'd love to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:12.360 --> 00:35:19.020
see more voter education and election officials&nbsp;
that are kind of really in the 21st century when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:19.020 --> 00:35:25.980
it comes to how they communicate with voters. I'd&nbsp;
love to see pre-registration, kids who can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:25.980 --> 00:35:34.640
registered at 16 and 17. And then I'd love to see&nbsp;
Congress finally restore the Voting Rights Act.

00:35:34.640 --> 00:35:37.140
BECKER: It's kind of a vicious circle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:37.140 --> 00:35:42.900
as our elected leaders at some levels are&nbsp;
less representative of our electorate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:43.440 --> 00:35:47.700
perhaps the diverse electorate sees less&nbsp;
of a connection between them and what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:47.700 --> 00:35:53.820
government is doing and they check out and don't&nbsp;
participate which leads to a furtherance of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:53.820 --> 00:35:59.940
less representative set of leaders. And to some&nbsp;
degree, we need to break that vicious cycle. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:59.940 --> 00:36:05.340
if we can get more people to turn out more&nbsp;
regularly—young people, people of color, people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.340 --> 00:36:09.840
who are not socio-economically advantaged—these&nbsp;
are all people who participate in much lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:09.840 --> 00:36:15.540
rates, we could see leaders getting elected that&nbsp;
represent their interests a little more and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.540 --> 00:36:19.500
turn those voters will continue to see that their&nbsp;
interests are represented and they may be more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:19.500 --> 00:36:25.500
inclined to participate. And maybe we get into a&nbsp;
much more constructive cycle if we can get there.

00:36:25.500 --> 00:36:31.980
SIERRA: It’s a hard thing to fix. Especially&nbsp;
in the United States, which is larger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:31.980 --> 00:36:36.480
messier, and more diverse than many of&nbsp;
the countries topping the turnout list.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:37.140 --> 00:36:40.860
But difficulty is never a good reason not to try.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:41.460 --> 00:36:48.060
Low voter turnout creates a gap between what U.S.&nbsp;
citizens want and what their government actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:48.060 --> 00:36:54.540
does. And that’s true of foreign policy just as&nbsp;
much as domestic policy. The more Americans vote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:54.540 --> 00:37:01.140
the more our policies on China, trade, climate&nbsp;
change, and all the rest reflect the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:01.140 --> 00:37:07.200
worldviews of American citizens. Low turnout also&nbsp;
undercuts our credibility on the world stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:07.200 --> 00:37:12.240
and it allows our adversaries to point out&nbsp;
hypocrisy when we support democracy abroad.

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:16.980
BECKER: I mean, I guess the last message I have&nbsp;
is one of optimism and hope. I mean, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:16.980 --> 00:37:20.400
we've not always done as well as we could&nbsp;
here in the United States. We have not always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.400 --> 00:37:24.900
had the level of participation that we could&nbsp;
expect. We have not always nominated leaders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:24.900 --> 00:37:33.300
who reflect our best selves. But we often&nbsp;
self-correct. And we often find ways to bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:38.520
back the best in ourselves at certain points&nbsp;
in time. And there have been times when we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:38.520 --> 00:37:43.860
seen a dark period followed by a period of hope&nbsp;
and a period of participation and engagement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:44.580 --> 00:37:50.100
One of the things I'm inspired by every day, as&nbsp;
a lot of us are working crazy hours in these last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:50.100 --> 00:37:55.920
few days of the election, is the American voter.&nbsp;
I'm supposed to be giving them information and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:55.920 --> 00:38:01.080
providing a better system for them, and instead&nbsp;
they're giving me inspiration every day. I see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:01.080 --> 00:38:05.520
lines showing up to vote early. I'm seeing people&nbsp;
returning mail ballots in rates they've never done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:05.520 --> 00:38:11.640
before. I see people educating themselves about&nbsp;
a process which is tough. They're in a pandemic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:11.640 --> 00:38:17.580
We're experiencing foreign interference and&nbsp;
disinformation. I'm continuously inspired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:17.580 --> 00:38:22.800
by their will to have their voice heard in&nbsp;
this election. I'm relatively confident that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:22.800 --> 00:38:28.140
we're going to see that heard loud and clear. And&nbsp;
hopefully within the few days after Election Day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:28.140 --> 00:38:30.900
we'll have a really clear direction&nbsp;
for this country that is hopeful.

00:38:30.900 --> 00:38:36.900
Sérgio Teixeira Jr.: Hello my name is Sérgio&nbsp;
Teixeira Jr. and I am a journalist from Brazil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:36.900 --> 00:38:42.840
Voting is mandatory in Brazil, between&nbsp;
the ages of 16 and 18 you are allowed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:42.840 --> 00:38:47.190
vote but you don’t have to. And in Brazil for&nbsp;
decades now we have been voting electronically.

00:38:47.190 --> 00:38:52.320
Alexandra: Hi my name is Alexandra and I’m&nbsp;
from Sweden. In Sweden elections always take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:52.320 --> 00:38:56.820
place on Sundays, which means that most people are&nbsp;
able to go and vote during the day without having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:56.820 --> 00:39:01.260
to take time off from work. People are encouraged&nbsp;
to vote and some schools even hold their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:01.260 --> 00:39:07.080
elections as part of an initiative called Skolval&nbsp;
or school election in which students get to vote.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:07.080 --> 00:39:11.520
These votes aren’t part of the official election,&nbsp;
but it gives many young people an insight into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:11.520 --> 00:39:15.840
voting process and there are often discussions and&nbsp;
events happening around these school elections.

00:39:18.200 --> 00:39:25.140
SIERRA: The time has come for me to tell our&nbsp;
American listeners to go vote. And that’s just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:25.140 --> 00:39:30.480
what I’m going to do. There’s not a single&nbsp;
global issue that we’ve discussed on Why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:35.640
It Matters that you don’t have the chance&nbsp;
to influence with your vote. Worried about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:35.640 --> 00:39:42.240
climate change? Vote about it. Want the world to&nbsp;
focus on pandemic readiness? Step into the booth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:42.240 --> 00:39:47.760
And when it comes to the problem of turnout,&nbsp;
your vote addresses the issue directly. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:47.760 --> 00:39:51.960
get to fix it, by voting yourself,&nbsp;
and by doing everything you can to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:51.960 --> 00:39:59.400
encourage those around you to do the same.&nbsp;
So do it. Enjoy it. It’s a powerful thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

